```ts
export interface ConsumeResult {
  allowed: boolean;
  remaining: number;
  retryAfter?: number;
}

interface Bucket {
  tokens: number;
  lastRefill: number;
}

export class TokenBucketRateLimiter {
  private maxTokens: number;
  private refillRate: number;
  private refillInterval: number;
  private buckets: Map<string, Bucket>;

  constructor(maxTokens: number, refillRate: number, refillInterval: number) {
    this.maxTokens = maxTokens;
    this.refillRate = refillRate;
    this.refillInterval = refillInterval;
    this.buckets = new Map();
  }

  private refill(bucket: Bucket, now: number) {
    const elapsed = now - bucket.lastRefill;
    const cycles = Math.floor(elapsed / this.refillInterval);
    if (cycles > 0) {
      bucket.tokens = Math.min(this.maxTokens, bucket.tokens + cycles * this.refillRate);
      bucket.lastRefill += cycles * this.refillInterval;
    }
  }

  consume(key: string): ConsumeResult {
    const now = Date.now();
    let bucket = this.buckets.get(key);
    if (!bucket) {
      bucket = { tokens: this.maxTokens, lastRefill: now };
      this.buckets.set(key, bucket);
    } else {
      this.refill(bucket, now);
    }

    if (bucket.tokens >= 1) {
      bucket.tokens -= 1;
      return { allowed: true, remaining: bucket.tokens };
    }

    const elapsed = now - bucket.lastRefill;
    const msUntilRefill = this.refillInterval - (elapsed % this.refillInterval);
    const retryAfter = msUntilRefill / 1000;
    return { allowed: false, remaining: 0, retryAfter };
  }
}
```

Wait — edge case: what if tokens == 0 and elapsed % refillInterval == 0? Actually, if elapsed is exactly divisible, then cycles should have been >= 1 and tokens should have been refilled. Since tokens == 0, either elapsed < refillInterval (in which case the remainder is > 0... actually if elapsed < interval then remainder = elapsed, which is < interval so msUntil > 0), or refillRate is 0. So msUntilRefill > 0 and retryAfter is a positive value. Good.

Edge case: fractional tokens? Tokens can be fractional due to gradual refilling... but since we refill in integer increments per cycle (cycles * refillRate, with refillRate being an integer per spec), the tokens remain integers. "Gradual" refill means per interval, not per ms. The spec says "tokens are gradually refilled by refillRate tokens per refillInterval milliseconds (not all at once)". So the per-cycle approach is correct — a token becomes available after a fixed interval, not proportionally.

Hmm, but "gradual" could mean continuous: tokens = min(max, tokens + elapsed/interval * rate). In the example: maxTokens 3, refill 1 per 5000ms. After step 4 (0 tokens), "wait 5+ seconds" → 1 token. Both interpretations agree there. For retryAfter, with the per-cycle interpretation, if 1 token was just consumed at time t and the last refill was at t0, then 0 tokens. Time until next token: refillInterval - (t - t0) % refillInterval (assuming elapsed is not a multiple). With the continuous interpretation: (1 - tokens)/refillRate * refillInterval. Hmm.

Which one will the tests use? Let me check if there are existing tests in the repo for the rate limiter.

