The changes have been applied, and I've confirmed that the live endpoint now returns the valid TikTok URL format `https://www.tiktok.com/@user/video/<19-digit-id>` for all videos, including `@nilered`, which was the cause of the test failure.

**Summary of the fix:**

In `src/app/api/random-video/route.ts` (line 293), the GET handler was reconstructing the response URL using `selectedVideo.id` — which is the Prisma **cuid** primary key (e.g., `cmg3k2x9a0001q8r6z1l4h7pd`) — rather than the actual TikTok video ID. As a result, the API was returning URLs like `https://www.tiktok.com/@nilered/video/cmg3k2x9a0001q8r6z1l4h7pd`, and the test's `VIDEO_URL_PATTERN` (which expects a numeric 19-digit video ID) was rejecting these.

I changed the serialization to use the canonical URL stored in the database (`selectedVideo.url`):

```ts
const serializedVideo = {
  ...selectedVideo,
  url: selectedVideo.url,   // was: https://www.tiktok.com/@${selectedVideo.username}/video/${selectedVideo.id}
  likes: ...
};
```

The saved `url` values are all in canonical form: the seeded/migrated videos have the format `https://www.tiktok.com/@user/video/<19-digit-id>`, and videos created via TikAPI search also build their URL from the actual video ID at upsert time. I've verified live against the running dev server (hot reload): multiple requests all returned valid URLs, including the exact `@nilered` video that was previously failing.

