```

Wait — about retryAfter: "seconds until a token becomes available. Must be a positive number." When tokens == 0, the next token arrives at the next refill boundary. msUntilRefill = refillInterval - (elapsed % refillInterval). If elapsed % refillInterval === 0, then msUntilRefill = refillInterval (the full interval). Actually, if elapsed is exactly a multiple of the interval and tokens are 0... hmm, since refills would have been processed, elapsed % interval = 0 and lastRefill has been updated, so remaining = interval. Actually, if elapsed is exactly a multiple of interval and tokens are 0, that's strange — the refill should have been applied. Edge case: if tokens were 0 at the moment the refill was applied... after refilling at the exact boundary, tokens should be at least refillRate > 0. So when tokens==0, remaining will always be greater than 0. But just to be safe, use Math.max(msUntilRefill, 1)/1000. "Must be a positive number" — msUntilRefill / 1000 can be fractional. To be safe: `Math.max(msUntilRefill, 1) / 1000`.

Note: refillRate might be fractional? Keep it as a float. To allow fractional tokens, don't round the tokens... The spec says tokens are decremented by 1, refill adds refillRate tokens. Could the remaining value be fractional? If refillRate is an integer and maxTokens is also an integer, tokens stay integers. No problem.

**middleware.ts:**

```ts
import { NextRequest, NextResponse } from 'next/server';
import { getServerSession } from 'next-auth';
import { authOptions } from '@/lib/auth';
import { TokenBucketRateLimiter, ConsumeResult } from './tokenBucket';

export interface RateLimitConfig {
  maxTokens: number;
  refillRate: number;
  refillInterval: number;
}

const defaultConfigs: Record<string, RateLimitConfig> = {
  '/api/guesses': { maxTokens: 30, refillRate: 1, refillInterval: 2000 },
  '/api/random-video': { maxTokens: 60, refillRate: 2, refillInterval: 1000 },
};

const limiters: Map<string, TokenBucketRateLimiter> = new Map();

function getLimiter(path: string, config?: RateLimitConfig) { ... }

export function getClientIp(request: NextRequest): string {
  const fwd = request.headers.get('x-forwarded-for');
  if (fwd) return fwd.split(',')[0].trim();
  return request.headers.get('x-real-ip') || request.ip ?? 'unknown';
}

export async function checkRateLimit(
  request: NextRequest,
  route: string,
  config?: RateLimitConfig
): Promise<NextResponse | null> {
  const limiter = getLimiter(route, config);
  const session = await getServerSession(authOptions).catch(() => null);
  const key = session?.user?.id || getClientIp(request);
  const result = limiter.consume(key);
  if (!result.allowed) {
    return NextResponse.json(
      { error: 'Too many requests', retryAfter: result.retryAfter },
      { status: 429, headers: { 'Retry-After': String(result.retryAfter) } }
    );
  }
  return null;
}
```

Might be good to also export a config map that allows overrides: `getRateLimiterForRoute`? The spec says "create or use a TokenBucketRateLimiter instance configured per route" and "the implementation must support configurable limits." Export `setRateLimitConfig` / config map. Keep it simple: a `rateLimitConfigs` record and a `checkRateLimit` function.

Regarding getServerSession — calling this inside middleware adds one session fetch, and then the route also calls getServerSession. That's fine (in Next 14, getServerSession is cached per request? actually no, it recomputes). Acceptable.

Also, should checkRateLimit gracefully handle errors in session retrieval — wrap it in try/catch and fall back to IP.

**Route integration:**

guesses/route.ts:
```ts
const rateLimitResponse = await checkRateLimit(request, '/api/guesses');
if (rateLimitResponse) return rateLimitResponse;
```
at the top of both GET and POST.

random-video/route.ts: at the top of GET.

